rid"><span class="enc-name">Qasim Barid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qasim Barid was a Turkic noble who became chief minister of the declining Bahmani Sultanate in the 1490s and controlled its last sultans from Bidar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad was a Zaydi scholar who proclaimed himself imam in the northern highlands of Yemen in 1597 and launched a revolt against Ottoman rule.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-qasim-ibn-muhammad-of-algeciras"><span class="enc-name">al-Qasim ibn Muhammad of Algeciras</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">al-Qasim ibn Muhammad was the last Hammudid ruler of the Taifa of Algeciras in southern al-Andalus, succeeding his father Muhammad ibn al-Qasim around 1048.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qasim-khan-of-qasim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qasim-khan-of-qasim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qasim Khan (Qasim Khanate)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qasim Khan was a son of Ulugh Muhammad, founder of the Kazan Khanate, who entered the service of Grand Prince Vasily II of Moscow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qasim-razvi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qasim-razvi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qasim Razvi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qasim Razvi was the leader of the Majlis-e-Ittehadul Muslimeen in Hyderabad State from 1946 and commander of its Razakar militia