an class="enc-name">Qatif</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qatif is an oasis town and port on the Gulf coast of eastern Saudi Arabia, facing the Bahrain islands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qatr-al-nada"><span class="enc-name">Qatr al-Nada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qatr al-Nada was the daughter of Khumarawayh, the Tulunid ruler of Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qatran-tabrizi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qatran-tabrizi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qatran Tabrizi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qatran Tabrizi was a Persian poet from Azerbaijan who served local rulers of north-western Iran and the Caucasus, including the Rawwadid emir Abu'l-Hayja Wahsudan and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qaus-gabri"><span class="enc-name">Qaus-gabri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qaus-gabri was a king of Edom in the early 7th century BCE, known from Assyrian records.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qavam-al-din-marashi"><span class="enc-name">Qavam al-Din Mar'ashi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qavam al-Din Mar'ashi, known as Mir-i Buzurg, was a Twelver Shia sayyid and Sufi teacher in Mazandaran whose dervish followers became a political force.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qavurt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qavurt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qavurt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qavurt was a Seljuk prince, son of Chaghri Beg and elder brother of Alp Arslan, who took Kerman in the 1040s and founded the Seljuk dynasty there.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qays"><span class="enc-name">Qays</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qays were the northern Arab tribal grouping that opposed the Yemeni or Qahtani faction in the early Islamic period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qazaghan"><span class="enc-name">Qazaghan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qazaghan was a Turkic amir of the Qara'unas who defeated and killed the Chagatai khan Qazan in 1346 or 1347 and became the real ruler of the western Chagatai Khanate in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qazan-khan-ibn-yasaur"><span class="enc-name">Qazan Khan ibn Yasaur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qazan Khan ibn Yasaur was khan of the Chagatai Khanate from about 1343 until his death in 1346 or 1347.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qazi"><span class="enc-name">Qazi</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Qazi is the form of the Arabic word qadi, meaning a judge in Islamic law, used in Persian and South Asian contexts.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qazi-muhammad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qazi-muhammad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qazi Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qazi Muhammad was a Kurdish judge and religious leader of Mahabad who led the Kurdistan Democratic Party and became president of the Soviet-backed Republic of Mahabad…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qena"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qena.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qena</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qena is a city in Upper Egypt on the east bank of the Nile, capital of Qena Governorate and close to the temple of Dendera.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qerri"><span class="enc-name">Qerri</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qerri was a town on the Nile in Sudan, north of the confluence of the Blue and White Niles.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qezel-ozan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qezel-ozan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qezel Ozan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qezel Ozan, the 'red river', is a river of north-western Iran that rises in Kurdistan province and flows through Zanjan province, then joins the Shahrud at Manjil…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qhapaq-nan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qhapaq-nan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qhapaq Ñan</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qhapaq Ñan was the road network of the Inca Empire, some 40,000 kilometres long, running along the Andes and the Pacific coast from present-day Colombia to Chile…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qi-jiguang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qi-jiguang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qi Jiguang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qi Jiguang was a Ming dynasty general who defeated the wokou pirates raiding the coasts of Zhejiang and Fujian in the 1550s and 1560s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qian-chu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qian-chu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qian Chu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qian Chu was the last king of Wuyue, the coastal kingdom around Hangzhou, reigning from 947 to 978.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qian-liu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qian-liu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qian Liu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qian Liu was the founder of Wuyue, one of the Ten Kingdoms of China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qianan"><span class="enc-name">Qian'an</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qian'an is a county-level city in Tangshan prefecture, north-eastern Hebei province, China, on the Luan River near the Great Wall.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qiang-historical-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qiang-historical-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qiang (historical people)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qiang were pastoral and farming peoples of north-western China, living in the regions of modern Gansu, Qinghai and western Sichuan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qianling-mausoleum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qianling-mausoleum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qianling Mausoleum</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qianling Mausoleum is the tomb complex of the Tang emperor Gaozong and his wife Wu Zetian, built into Mount Liang in Qian County, north-west of Xi'an in Shaanxi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qianlong-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qianlong Emperor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qianlong Emperor ruled the Qing Empire from 1735 to 1796, presiding over its greatest territorial extent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qiantang-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qiantang-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qiantang River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qiantang River is the main river of Zhejiang province in eastern China, flowing past Hangzhou into Hangzhou Bay and the East China Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qiao-zong"><span class="enc-name">Qiao Zong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qiao Zong was an official who rebelled against the Eastern Jin in Sichuan in 405 and founded the state of Western Shu, ruling from Chengdu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qifu-clan"><span class="enc-name">Qifu clan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qifu were a Xianbei clan who settled around the upper Yellow River in present-day Gansu in the 3rd and 4th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qifu-gangui"><span class="enc-name">Qifu Gangui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qifu Gangui was the second ruler of Western Qin, a Xianbei state in Gansu during the Sixteen Kingdoms period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qifu-guoren"><span class="enc-name">Qifu Guoren</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qifu Guoren was a chief of the Qifu clan of the Xianbei who founded Western Qin in 385, after the collapse of Former Qin following the Battle of Fei River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qifu-mumo"><span class="enc-name">Qifu Mumo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qifu Mumo was the last ruler of the Western Qin, one of the Sixteen Kingdoms of northern China, reigning from 428 to 431.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qilian-mountains"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qilian-mountains.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qilian Mountains</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qilian Mountains are a range on the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan Plateau, along the border of Gansu and Qinghai in China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qimin-khagan"><span class="enc-name">Qimin Khagan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qimin Khagan was a ruler of the Eastern Turkic Khaganate from 599 to 609.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qin-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qin dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qin dynasty was the first imperial dynasty of China, which unified the country in 221 BCE under Qin Shi Huang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qin-er-shi"><span class="enc-name">Qin Er Shi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qin Er Shi, personal name Huhai, was the second emperor of the Qin dynasty, reigning from 210 to 207 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qin-shi-huang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qin Shi Huang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qin Shi Huang, born Ying Zheng, became king of the State of Qin in 246 BCE and between 230 and 221 BCE conquered the six rival Warring States, unifying China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qin-zhong"><span class="enc-name">Qin Zhong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qin Zhong was a leader of the Qin people in the ninth century BCE, ruling from about 844 to 822 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qing-conquest-of-taiwan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qing-conquest-of-taiwan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qing conquest of Taiwan</span><span class="enc-type">Campaign · 1683</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qing conquest of Taiwan took place in 1683, when Qing naval forces under Shi Lang defeated the Kingdom of Tungning ruled by the descendants of Koxinga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qing-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qing Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qing Empire was the last imperial dynasty of China, founded by the Manchus, who conquered Ming China from 1644 and ruled until 1912.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qinghai-lake.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qinghai Lake</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qinghai Lake, also known by its Mongolian name Kokonor, is the largest lake in China, a salt lake on the north-eastern Tibetan Plateau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qingyang"><span class="enc-name">Qingyang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qingyang is a city in the east of Gansu province, China, on the Loess Plateau near the borders of Shaanxi and Ningxia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qingzhou"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qingzhou.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qingzhou</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qingzhou is a city in central Shandong, China, named after one of the ancient Nine Provinces and an important regional capital from the Han to the Ming.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qiniq"><span class="enc-name">Qiniq</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Qiniq were a clan of the Oghuz Turks of Central Asia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qinnasrin"><span class="enc-name">Qinnasrin</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qinnasrin was an ancient city in northern Syria, south-west of Aleppo, known in Greek and Roman times as Chalcis ad Belum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qirwash-ibn-al-muqallad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qirwash-ibn-al-muqallad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qirwash ibn al-Muqallad was the Uqaylid emir of Mosul and the most important ruler of the Arab Uqaylid dynasty, reigning from 1001 to about 1050.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/qishn"><span class="enc-name">Qishn</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Qishn is a coastal town in Al Mahrah Governorate in eastern Yemen, on the Arabian Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/qizil-arslan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/qizil-arslan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Qizil Arslan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Qizil Arslan was an atabeg of the Eldiguzid dynasty of Azerbaijan, the son of Eldiguz, who succeeded his brother Jahan Pahlavan in 1186.</span></a></li></ul>
